--TEST--
Core\Math::abs, sign, min, max and clamp answer over both arms of `int|float`
--FILE--
<?nvs
echo Core\Math::abs(-7), "|", Core\Math::abs(7), "|", Core\Math::abs(-7.5), "\n";
// `sign` is `$n <=> 0`, and `-0.0` is zero. That both members are the ordering
// trio written another way on every row, and that each parts from it only at
// the one value it refuses, is swept in
// math-abs-and-sign-are-the-ordering-trio-and-part-from-it-only-where-they-refuse.nvst.
echo Core\Math::sign(-3), "|", Core\Math::sign(0), "|", Core\Math::sign(2.5), "|",
     Core\Math::sign(-0.0), "\n";

// One ordering for two loose values and for an array, so the two members
// cannot disagree — `Core\Arr::min` walks the same comparison.
echo Core\Math::min(3, 9), "|", Core\Math::max(3, 9), "\n";
echo Core\Math::min("pear", "apple"), "|", Core\Math::max("pear", "apple"), "\n";

echo Core\Math::clamp(42, 0, 10), "|", Core\Math::clamp(-42, 0, 10), "|",
     Core\Math::clamp(5, 0, 10), "\n";

// `int`'s smallest value has no `int` magnitude, and PHP's answer — becoming a
// `float` by itself — is what ADR 0007 forbids.
try {
    echo Core\Math::abs(-9223372036854775807 - 1), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "abs: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
// An empty range has no value to clamp into.
try {
    echo Core\Math::clamp(5, 10, 1), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "clamp: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
// `NaN` is on neither side of zero.
try {
    echo Core\Math::sign(Core\Math::sqrt(-1.0)), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "sign: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
7|7|7.5
-1|0|1|0
3|9
apple|pear
10|0|5
abs: Core\Math::abs has no `int` answer for `int`'s smallest value: its magnitude is one past the largest
clamp: Core\Math::clamp was given a `low` above its `high`, which is an empty range
sign: Core\Math::sign has no answer for `NaN`: it is on neither side of zero
